# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
C={'01': 'The passages examined place marital and bridal language within Israel’s covenant and prophetic setting. Jeremiah describes the LORD as Israel’s husband, Isaiah calls Israel’s Maker her husband, Hosea promises renewed betrothal after judgment, and Isaiah connects Zion and Jerusalem with marriage and bridal imagery (Jeremiah 31:31–32; Isaiah 54:5; Hosea 2:19–20; Isaiah 62:4–5). Revelation 19 announces the marriage of the Lamb and states that His wife has made herself ready, while Revelation 21 explicitly introduces “the bride, the Lamb’s wife” and then shows John the holy Jerusalem, whose gates bear the names of Israel’s twelve tribes and whose foundations bear the names of the twelve apostles (Revelation 19:7–9; 21:9–14). Paul uses genuine marriage and espousal imagery concerning Christ and believers in Ephesians 5 and 2 Corinthians 11, but neither passage explicitly calls the Body of Christ the Bride. Paul instead identifies the Church as Christ’s Body, one new man, and one Body, and describes believers as blessed and seated in heavenly places in Christ (Ephesians 1:3, 22–23; 2:6, 14–16; 3:6; 4:4). Taken together, these passages retain bridal identity within Israel’s prophetic and Jerusalem-centered setting while identifying the Mystery company as Christ’s Body with the heavenly position and calling Paul describes.', '02': 'The gospel of the kingdom announces the kingdom promised in Israel’s prophetic Scriptures and proclaimed as at hand by John the Baptist, Jesus, and the Twelve. The prophets promised Israel a coming King and kingdom (Isaiah 9:6–7; Jeremiah 23:5–6; Daniel 2:44), and John, Jesus, and the Twelve announced that this kingdom was at hand, with the Twelve sent specifically to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (Matthew 3:2; 4:17; 10:5–7). After the resurrection, the apostles still asked about the restoration of the kingdom to Israel (Acts 1:6), and Peter’s appeal in Acts 2–3 rests on what God had spoken through “all his holy prophets” (Acts 3:21). Paul, by contrast, explicitly calls his own message “the gospel of the grace of God” (Acts 20:24), and his reasoning from Israel’s Scriptures in the synagogues does not alter that commission. The passages associate the kingdom proclamation with the response God required of its hearers, including repentance and baptism, but they do not identify those responses as the content of the kingdom being announced. Matthew 24:14 further speaks of “this gospel of the kingdom” being preached before the end. Taken together, the passages present the gospel of the kingdom as the announcement of Israel’s promised King and kingdom, while Paul explicitly identifies his ministry with “the gospel of the grace of God.”', '03': 'The passages examined place the future prophetic Tribulation within Israel’s identified prophetic setting, while Paul describes believers as not appointed to the coming wrath and as gathered to Christ. Daniel fixes the seventy weeks upon Daniel’s people and holy city, Jeremiah calls the coming distress “the time of Jacob’s trouble,” and Matthew places the great Tribulation in the setting of Daniel’s abomination of desolation, Judaea, and the visible coming of the Son of Man (Daniel 9:24–27; Jeremiah 30:7; Matthew 24:15–30). Revelation likewise identifies the sealed 144,000 according to the tribes of Israel. Paul states that believers are “not appointed...to wrath,” describes Jesus as the One who delivers them “from the wrath to come,” and describes the dead in Christ rising and living believers being caught up with them to meet the Lord in the air (1 Thessalonians 1:10; 4:16–17; 5:9). First Corinthians 15:51–52 identifies the transformation of living believers as a mystery, while Philippians 3:20 identifies their citizenship as heavenly. Present Christian suffering is therefore distinguishable from the prophetic wrath described in the Tribulation passages. The interpretation of the “falling away” and the restrainer in 2 Thessalonians 2 remains disputed, and the preceding findings do not depend upon resolving those questions. Taken together, these passages place the Tribulation within Israel’s prophetic timetable while presenting the Body’s expectation as gathering to Christ, heavenly transformation, and deliverance from the coming wrath.', '04': 'The passages examined distinguish Israel and the Body of Christ by the names, covenants, promises, relationships, and expectations they explicitly assign to each. Peter describes the prophetic hope as that which God had “spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began” (Acts 3:21), while Paul describes the mystery as having been “kept secret since the world began” (Romans 16:25). Israel is identified as a nation with its origin in the promises to Abraham (Exodus 19:6; Romans 9:4–5), while Paul identifies the church as Christ’s Body, with Christ as its head (Ephesians 1:22–23). Israel’s covenants are named with the house of Israel and the house of Judah (Jeremiah 31:31), while Gentiles are described as formerly “strangers from the covenants of promise” and then “made nigh by the blood of Christ” (Ephesians 2:12–13). Israel’s passages speak of land, restoration, Jerusalem, and David’s throne; Paul describes believers as blessed “in heavenly places in Christ,” as displaying God’s wisdom through the church, and as awaiting their gathering to Christ and appearing with Him in glory (Ephesians 1:3; 3:10; 1 Thessalonians 4:17; Colossians 3:4). Taken together, these passages continue to identify Israel according to Israel’s stated national and covenantal promises and the church as Christ’s Body with the heavenly standing and hope Paul describes.', '05': 'The passages examined describe a consistent heavenly pattern for the Body of Christ. Paul identifies the church as Christ’s Body with Christ as its Head (Ephesians 1:22–23), describes believers as raised and seated together “in heavenly places in Christ Jesus” (Ephesians 2:6), and says they are blessed with “all spiritual blessings in heavenly places in Christ” (Ephesians 1:3). He identifies their citizenship as being in heaven, from which they await the Savior and the transformation of their bodies (Philippians 3:20–21), while Colossians describes their life as presently hidden with Christ and their future as appearing with Him in glory (Colossians 3:3–4). First Thessalonians 4:16–17 adds the resurrection of the dead in Christ, the catching up of living believers, and their enduring presence with the Lord. Ephesians further places the church’s spiritual conflict and the display of God’s manifold wisdom within the heavenly sphere (Ephesians 3:10; 6:12). These passages describe a present position as well as a future manifestation, and they do not reduce the heavenly calling merely to a future destination. Taken together, these passages present the Body’s heavenly calling as a Christ-centered identity, position, blessing, citizenship, hope, conflict, and purpose grounded in union with Christ.', '06': 'The passages examined define the Mystery by its revelatory history: it was “kept secret since the world began,” “hid in God,” and “hid from ages and from generations,” but was subsequently revealed (Romans 16:25; Ephesians 3:9; Colossians 1:26). Paul states that he received his message by revelation from Jesus Christ and that the Mystery was made known to him by revelation (Galatians 1:12; Ephesians 3:3), while Ephesians 3:5 also states that it was revealed by the Spirit to holy apostles and prophets. Ephesians identifies Jew and Gentile as fellow heirs and members of “the same body,” reconciled to God in one Body through the cross (Ephesians 2:14–16; 3:6), while Paul describes that Body as blessed and seated in heavenly places in Christ (Ephesians 1:3; 2:6). Colossians adds “Christ in you, the hope of glory,” and 1 Corinthians 15 identifies the transformation of living believers as a mystery (Colossians 1:27; 1 Corinthians 15:51–52). Ephesians 3:10 further states that through the church God’s manifold wisdom is made known to principalities and powers in heavenly places. These statements stand in contrast to the prophetic revelation Peter describes as that which God had spoken through His prophets since the world began (Acts 3:21). Taken together, these passages present the Mystery as a previously hidden purpose of God in Christ, revealed through Paul, concerning the one Body and its union with Christ, heavenly position, hope, and place in God’s revealed purpose.', '07': 'The passages examined describe the Day of the Lord within the prophetic Scriptures as a time of divine intervention marked by wrath, darkness, judgment, cosmic disturbance, and judgment involving Israel and the nations (Isaiah 13:6–13; Joel 2:1–11; Amos 5:18–20; Zephaniah 1:14–18). Zechariah 14 places the Lord’s visible intervention within that prophetic setting and describes His feet standing upon the Mount of Olives, while the larger prophetic record moves from judgment toward deliverance, restoration, and Messiah’s reign. Paul likewise says that the Day comes “as a thief in the night” and that sudden destruction comes upon “them,” but immediately distinguishes his readers as “brethren” who are “not in darkness” and are “not appointed...to wrath” (1 Thessalonians 5:2–9). In the preceding passage, Paul describes the dead in Christ rising and living believers being caught up with them to meet the Lord in the air and “ever be with the Lord” (1 Thessalonians 4:16–17). He also describes Jesus as the One who delivers believers “from the wrath to come” (1 Thessalonians 1:10). The interpretation of details in 2 Thessalonians 2, including the textual variation concerning “the day of Christ” or “the day of the Lord” and questions surrounding the chapter’s chronology, remains disputed; the findings above do not depend upon resolving those questions. Taken together, these passages present the Day of the Lord within Israel’s prophetic setting of judgment moving toward restoration, while Paul presents believers as awaiting their gathering to Christ and as not appointed to the wrath associated with the coming Day.', '08': 'The passages examined explicitly associate the Day of Christ with God’s continuing work toward completion, sincerity and blamelessness, confidence unto the end, and future rejoicing concerning faithful labor. Philippians 1:6 states that God will perform the good work He began until the Day of Jesus Christ; Philippians 1:10 directs believers toward sincerity and being without offence until the Day of Christ; Philippians 2:16 connects that Day with Paul’s rejoicing concerning his labor; and 1 Corinthians 1:8 speaks of confirmation unto the end and blamelessness in the Day of our Lord Jesus Christ. Other Pauline passages separately describe the Body’s future gathering to Christ, resurrection and transformation, appearance with Christ in glory, accountability before the Judgment Seat of Christ, and presentation of the church to Christ (1 Thessalonians 4:16–17; 1 Corinthians 15:51–52; Colossians 3:4; Romans 14:10; 2 Corinthians 5:10; Ephesians 5:27). These related doctrines contribute to the larger Pauline hope but are not individually called the Day of Christ in the passages that establish them. The wording of 2 Thessalonians 2:2 remains disputed between “day of Christ” and “day of the Lord,” and the conclusions above do not depend upon resolving that textual question. Taken together, these passages present the Day of Christ as a future Christ-centered horizon of completion, blamelessness, confidence, and rejoicing, within the larger Pauline hope of gathering, transformation, accountability, presentation, and glory with Christ.', '09': 'The passages examined present the remnant within Israel as evidence of God’s preserving faithfulness amid unbelief and judgment. In Elijah’s day, God preserved seven thousand in Israel who had not bowed to Baal (1 Kings 19:18), and the prophets repeatedly describe a remnant preserved within Israel through judgment (Isaiah 10:20–22; Jeremiah 23:3). Paul applies that pattern directly in Romans 11, where he asks whether God has cast away His people, answers “God forbid,” identifies himself as an Israelite, and states that “at this present time also there is a remnant according to the election of grace” (Romans 11:1–5). The remnant therefore remains a remnant of the people Paul continues to identify as Israel; its preservation does not create a new national identity. Romans 11 also distinguishes Israel and Gentiles in the olive-tree illustration and warns Gentiles against boasting, while Revelation 7 continues to identify the 144,000 according to the tribes of Israel. The remnant does not make God faithful; its preservation bears witness to the faithfulness of the God who has not cast away His people. Taken together, these passages present Israel’s remnant as a preserved believing portion within Israel through which judgment does not erase Israel’s identity or overturn God’s continuing purpose concerning the people He names as His own.', '10': 'The passages examined place Daniel’s seventy-week prophecy upon Daniel’s people and holy city, Israel and Jerusalem (Daniel 9:24). Daniel divides the prophecy so that sixty-nine weeks lead to Messiah, after which Messiah is cut off and the city and sanctuary are destroyed, while verse 27 describes a remaining final week (Daniel 9:25–27). Within that week, Daniel identifies a covenant confirmed with many, the continuation of sacrifice and offering, and a midpoint at which those sacrifices cease and desolation comes into view. Jesus later directs His hearers to Daniel’s abomination of desolation and states that great tribulation follows it, placing the Great Tribulation after the midpoint rather than making it synonymous with the entire seventieth week (Matthew 24:15–21). Scripture elsewhere uses corresponding three-and-one-half-year expressions that contribute to the prophetic description of this latter period, while Matthew 24 moves from the tribulation to the visible appearing of the Son of Man (Matthew 24:29–31). The prophecy consistently retains Israel and Jerusalem as its stated setting; the Body of Christ is not named as a subject of Daniel’s seventy weeks. Taken together, these passages present Daniel’s seventieth week as the remaining final period of the seventy-week prophecy determined upon Israel and Jerusalem, moving through its marked midpoint and Great Tribulation toward Messiah’s appearing and the fulfillment of God’s prophetic purpose concerning Israel.'}
